WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:01.770
JENNY BROWN: Choosing
the right database

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:04.740
can feel like a never
ending series of questions.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.020
Structured or unstructured data?

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.060
Scale or consistency?

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.220
Relational or nonrelational?

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.340
And wait-- what
about reliability?

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.800
Stay tuned to see how
Critter Junction narrows

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.782
the scope of their search to
get some much needed answers.

00:00:20.782 --> 00:00:24.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.000
When it comes time to
choose the right database

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.750
for your application needs,
it's all about trade-offs.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.760
Each database product
you'll run into

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.280
has plenty of
differentiators and features,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.140
all designed to help
you find the best

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.000
fit for your situation--

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.150
which on one side,
is good, but can also

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.400
lead to analysis paralysis.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.950
And this is where Critter
Junction found themselves

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.540
when we met up with them.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.570
They were looking for a database
for a new web and mobile mini

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.590
game.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.830
Fortunately, while looking at
the cascade of database options

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.080
in Google Cloud, we
helped them match up

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.510
each product to their
potential business needs

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.100
by focusing heavily on vertical
versus horizontal scaling.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.720
Narrowing the
scope of the search

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.910
was helpful for Critter
Junction and let

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.150
them evaluate their options
through the lens of scale.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:20.070
First, we took a look at SQL, or
more specifically, Cloud SQL--

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.390
a managed database offering
for MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.020
Server--

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.710
a modern offering for a
tried and true classic.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.250
Cloud SQL is a great fit for
any application with loads

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.930
of structured data
that's looking

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.900
for that sweet spot of
convenience, configuration

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.220
options, and performance.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.970
The familiarity alone could
make this a helpful option

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.260
for Critter Junction.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.250
But again, for this search,
we're focusing on scaling.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.640
The best part is that Cloud
SQL gives you flexible control

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.680
over scaling and deployment.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.510
It'll wait for
you to decide when

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.300
and how to grow with
high availability

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.580
and replication built in.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.200
Whether you need
vertical scaling

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.020
to instances with
higher CPU and memory

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.410
or adding read replicas to
achieve horizontal scaling,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.180
it's all up to you.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.370
But since Critter
Junction's elite gamers

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.100
couldn't handle any
downtime during resizing

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.340
and need to be able to fetch the
latest updates from each read

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.090
replica, Cloud SQL wasn't
the perfect choice.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.300
And once you reach a certain
database size or 4,000

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.310
concurrent connections,
it might be

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.780
time to look at another option.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.540
So let's turn things up a notch.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.190
When you want the ability to
go from small to massive scale,

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.930
Spanner is your answer.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.960
As a fully managed
relational database service,

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.260
it handles nearly unlimited
horizontal scaling for you.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.370
It's also got automatic data
sharding and replication,

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:52.020
giving you that 99.999%
uptime that your data admins

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.050
and users are so fond of.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.750
And while Spanner is the
backbone of billion user

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.210
services like Gmail
and YouTube, it

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.030
might be a little too powerful
for Critter Junction's

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.990
new minigame alone.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.910
So let's reel it back in a
bit to find a better match.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.340
Cloud Firestore sits
a little further

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.620
towards the opposite
end of the spectrum.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.870
It'll handle horizontal
scaling for you,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.000
but where Spanner focuses
on global consistency,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.870
availability, and
volume, Firestore also

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.510
focuses on developer
flexibility.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.630
Specifically, Firestore
can sync your data directly

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.810
with client applications
through real-time listeners

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.870
and support your hierarchical
data structures with real time

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.540
querying and offline support.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.530
Firestore scales up
automatically with no downtime.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.690
This lets Firestore
handle thousands

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.350
of requests per second, millions
of concurrent connections,

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.760
automatic data replication,
and strong consistency.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.310
This means Firestore could
offer the flexibility

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.530
that our Critter
Junction game devs need,

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.730
while also giving them the scale
for their application size.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.310
Sounds like a win for Firestore.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.680
While Firestore might be
great to handle things

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.130
like configurations, friends
lists, and game services,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.680
it's a novice when
it comes to big data.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.820
And for Critter
Junction's analytics team,

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.690
that's a big deal, as they
need a separate database

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.370
system for their needs.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.339
But that's something we'll talk
about in the next episode, when

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.800
we dive deeper into
their full architecture

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.940
and explore Spanner, Big Table,
and Memorystore, as well.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.220
Even though we've only
scratched the surface,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.980
Critter Junction already has
a pretty solid starting point

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.840
with Firestore for
their mini game

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.060
and is ready to start
designing a larger

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.410
system to suit their needs.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.280
Next time, we'll show you how
they grew their architecture

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.890
and database
solutions from here.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.240
Check out the links
below to learn more.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.090
And remember, always
be architecting.

